<section class="content">
    <h2>Why Bear Conservation Matters</h2>

    <p>
        Bears play a vital role in maintaining healthy ecosystems around the world.
        As large omnivores, they help control populations of other animals and spread seeds through their movement,
        contributing to forest regeneration and biodiversity. Without bears, many ecosystems would become unbalanced.
    </p>

    <p>
        However, all bear species today face increasing threats from human activity and environmental change.
        Protecting bears is not only about saving a single species, but about preserving entire ecosystems
        that depend on their presence.
    </p>
</section>

<!-- THREATS GRID -->
<section class="features">

    <div class="feature-box">
        <h3>🌍 Habitat Loss</h3>
        <p>
            Forests are being cut down for agriculture, cities, and industry,
            reducing the natural living space of bears worldwide.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-box">
        <h3>🌡 Climate Change</h3>
        <p>
            Rising temperatures affect polar ice and food availability,
            especially for species like the polar bear.
        </p>
    </div>

    <div class="feature-box">
        <h3>🏗 Human Conflict</h3>
        <p>
            Bears sometimes enter human areas in search of food,
            which leads to dangerous encounters for both humans and animals.
        </p>
    </div>

</section>

<!-- CONSERVATION EFFORTS -->
<section class="content">

    <h2>Global Conservation Efforts</h2>

    <p>
        Around the world, governments and organizations are working to protect bears through
        wildlife reserves, anti-poaching laws, and habitat restoration projects.
        Many protected areas have been created specifically to ensure bears can live and reproduce safely.
    </p>

    <p>
        Organizations such as WWF and local wildlife agencies monitor populations,
        educate communities, and help reduce human-wildlife conflict.
        These efforts have already helped increase populations of some species like the giant panda.
    </p>
</section>

